  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper level ridging extends over the Northern Rockers and into the \nNorthern Plains. Our area is still on the northeast side of the \nridge behind the departing upper low that is currently centered near \nthe Middle Mississippi River Valley/western Ohio River Valley. The \nmean ridge axis should continue to build eastward tonight into \nSunday with lee troughing to the west downstream of the next \nstronger trough over the Pacific NW/southwest Canada. We end up with \na relatively high spread in temperatures as the warmer air mass will \ntend to align along the main surface trough in central ND northwest \nnortheast ND ahead of a cold front expected to arrive Monday as the \nmain trough moves east. Temperatures in the warmer air mass to the \nwest will tend to be 90+ with at least a chance for brief advisory \ntype heat impacts in the western Devils Lake Basin (Heat Index \nValues near 100 and Wet Bulb Globe temperatures 80+). \n\nSoutherly flow does increase Sunday resulting in breezy to windy \nconditions, however the warm nose aloft in the 5-9kft layer will tend \nto limit mixing potential and highest gusts should remain under 40 \nmph along/west of the Red River Valley. Higher Tds to the east and \nsouth also limit fire weather concerns (RH would remain 40-55%) with \nlowest RH (30-35%) where HRB values remain higher in ND. \n\nThe best chances for wetting rain will be associated with the \npassage of this trough and cold front Monday with the probability for \n0.5\"+ highest north of Highway 2 in MN. This trough eventually \nstalls over the Hudson Bay region and the larger scale ridge becomes \nflattened, keeping our region in either northwest or westerly flow. \nThis allows for temperatures to trend towards either side of \nseasonal ranges (+/- 1 standard deviation of average). It also allows \nfor a least conditional chances for showers/thunderstorms next \nweek as progressive/weaker shortwaves move either from Canada in NW \nflow or from the Pacific NW in westerly flow. The most consistent \nsignal for the next period of precipitation with these features is \nlate Wednesday into Thursday, though there remains high variability \nlowering chances for measurable rain and confidence in impacts. \n\n...Severe Weather Risk Sunday Night...\n\nInitially capping should limit the potential for thunderstorm \ninitiation locally Sunday, however there is a signal with CAMs at \nthis range for a few storms to develop near the convergence \nzone/surface trough axis in central ND which may allow for a \nstrong/severe thunderstorm to develop towards our far northwest in \nthe evening hours (marginal cape and shear environment). Better \nsignal for thunderstorm arrives late as at least some PVA occurs \nahead of the main trough and front arrival late Sunday night/early \nMonday morning (generally 3-9am). Elevated instability and \nincreasing effective shear could support a large hail/damaging \nwind threat with thunderstorms that develop. \n\n...Severe Weather Risk Monday...\n\nInitially there is a lower risk for ongoing strong/marginally\nsevere storms early Monday morning, though activity may remain \nhighly elevated and marginal CAPE/shear lowers confidence in \nimpacts initially. The main result of any ongoing convection \nwould be what lingering impact mid level clouds have on \nconvective evolution Monday afternoon/evening. Better forcing \nwith the main wave favors the north while the cold front passing\nnorthwest to southeast places the region of better BL moisture \npooling/instability in the warm sector to our south-southeast. \nCloud cover could delay initiation in the region with more \nrobust instability and shear ahead of the front where capping \nmay already be playing a role in limiting local initiation. This\nis even more accounting for the signal for increasing smoke \naloft from the Pacific NW arriving with the trough.\n \nThe presence of broader forcing/convergence and a window where\ngood instability and strong shear will be present at least \ncreates a scenario for supercells or more organized severe \nclusters, so this will need to be monitored.",
